          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"rostand-cyrano-de-bergerac-019f33d6fc3682ce\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.98,\"analysis\":\"Roxane directly avows a past stage of her love: she loved the addressee for his beauty and soul together. The utterance presents an emotional state as true and helps narrate its development, but it does not itself undertake a commitment. Its poetic and impassioned delivery supplies no independent evidence of expressive/reflex-like discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"Roxane addresses Christian, but the surrounding dramatic irony divides the qualities she loves between Christian's beauty and Cyrano's words or perceived soul. This complicates the effective object of love without changing the utterance's strongly truth-conditional force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Roxane directly tells Christian, in a retrospective imperfect construction, that she loved him for his beauty and soul together.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The line forms the middle step in Roxane's account of her changing love: first Christian's beauty alone, then beauty and soul together, and now the soul alone. She sincerely presents this progression to Christian, unaware that the letters and eloquent language through which she apprehends his soul came from Cyrano. The resulting dramatic irony concerns mistaken attribution, not the core utterance's mode.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"« Ta beauté m'arrêtant, ton âme m'entraînant, / Je t'aimais pour les deux ensemble ! »\",\"supports\":\"The utterance explicitly presents Roxane's former love, and its dual grounds in beauty and soul, as a true emotional state.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Roxane contrasts having first loved Christian « pour ta seule beauté » with now loving him « pour ton âme » alone.\",\"supports\":\"The highlighted imperfect is a retrospective description of an intermediate emotional state, strongly supporting T rather than an undertaking or reflexive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The preceding dialogue reveals that Cyrano wrote the letters attributed to Christian; Christian later concludes, « C'est toi qu'elle aime ! »\",\"supports\":\"This establishes the dramatic irony and ambiguity concerning whose soul Roxane effectively loves while leaving her avowal's truth-conditional mode intact.\",\"confidence\":0.98}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core as a retrospective avowal of love. The mistaken attribution and dramatic irony are adequately treated as context, with no residual core force requiring P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
